WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.029
Film Courage: Now I know you are raising money
for the film on an equity crowdfunding site

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:08.300
and you’ve briefed us a little bit behind
the scenes that there are certain things you

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.650
can’t talk about.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.610
Forgive me if I overstep the bounds.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.350
So please stop me if at any moment if it’s
something [that I should not ask].

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:18.269
Because I am used to interviewing people who
have been on Kickstarter or Indiegogo [and

00:00:18.269 --> 00:00:19.769
Seed &amp; Spark].

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:23.810
So what is equity crowdfunding first and foremost?

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:27.060
How is it different from Kickstarter and Indiegogo?

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.019
David Willis: Well it’s a new law that has
just come into effect.

00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:31.259
It’s only been around for year.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:40.340
It went into effect in 2016 and it’s called
Regulation Crowdfunding or Equity Crowdfunding.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:47.470
And before this law came into effect if you
wanted to invest in a startup (like Facebook)

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:52.540
when they were first starting up, legally
you had to be what they call a high net worth

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.540
individual.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.330
You had to be somebody where you make over
$200,000 a year or you have a million dollars

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:58.840
in net worth.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.850
And that left out 90% of the population.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.600
90% of the population could not invest in
these.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.200
Only venture capitalist and really wealthy
people could do it.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:18.530
But this new law came into effect that allows
other businesses to raise money to everyone.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:22.990
The average person can invest with something
in equity crowdfunding and you couldn’t

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:23.990
do that before.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:29.229
The differences between this and Kickstarter
is when you put money into an equity crowdfunding

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:35.219
project like ours, if you did it on Kickstarter
they’d give you a DVD or a t-shirt or something

00:01:35.219 --> 00:01:36.219
like that.

00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:38.310
But with this you get a piece of the company.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.170
If the movie makes money, you make money.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:47.459
So you enjoy the success or failure (if the
movie fails of course, movies can always crash

00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:48.459
and burn.

00:01:48.459 --> 00:01:49.780
Anything can crash and burn).

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.530
If it succeeds, you go along for the ride.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.329
So that’s the big difference.

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:56.799
A lot of companies have been doing it.

00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:02.509
A lot of micro-breweries, restaurants, coffee
companies have all successfully raised millions

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:03.869
of dollars.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:06.700
Electric cars, there are a lot of electric
cars out there.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:13.620
There is a guy who successfully raised millions
of dollars for a jet pack, the first non-military

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.620
jet pack.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:15.620
Film Courage: That’s good to have!

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:16.620
David Willis: Yeah!

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.620
It’s so cool.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:18.959
They have the video, it’s real.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:19.959
It works.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:24.350
And you can put money into that company.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.450
There is tampon of the month club.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:27.450
Film Courage: Oh!

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:28.450
Good to know.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.310
David Willis: There is everything you can
possibly imagine.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:37.780
There is somebody starting up a company under
this new law We are the first feature-length

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.640
narrative film to raise money this way.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.930
There have been a couple in Europe.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:49.519
A couple in Asia, but we’re the first ones
in America.

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:55.430
One other company did a collection of short
films, but we’re the first ones to do a

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:56.460
feature-length narrative film.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:57.640
And it’s really exciting!

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.439
It’s cool because it’s new.

00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:03.190
Nobody has done it so it’s really exciting
and we’re succeeding.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.650
It’s going well.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.260
Film Courage: It is.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.420
I don’t know if you’re allowed…now forgive
me if I’m overstepping my bounds (we can

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.379
cut this out), but are you allowed to say
the dollar amount?

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:16.099
You’ve done very well since April 2017 is
when you launched the campaign.

00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:17.099
David Willis: Yes.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:18.250
We are doing well.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.540
The SEC (Security and Exchange Commission)
regulates this offering.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.849
It’s not like Kickstarter, where anybody
can just do it.

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:26.159
There’s a lot of paperwork involved in us
making this offering.

00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:27.909
And they have rules about what we can talk
about.

00:03:27.909 --> 00:03:30.780
And we can’t actually talk about dollar
amounts.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.530
People have to visit the website and all of
the information is right there.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.879
It’s super easy to read and all of that
is right there.

00:03:35.879 --> 00:03:38.269
But I can tell you this.

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:43.120
For a year previously before we did the equity
crowdfunding campaign, I went around and talked

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.450
to rich people, people outside of the [entertainment]
business, people who were wealthy.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.859
And I kind of tried to talk to them to raise
money.

00:03:49.859 --> 00:03:51.180
And I raised a little money.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.610
It took me a year.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:04:05.870
In 7 weeks I have raised 140% of what I raised
in a year talking to rich people.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:13.969
So I love equity crowdfunding because instead
of having to convince a few individuals with

00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:19.030
money to write a check, you can talk to the
whole world (international investors).

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:24.199
We have investors from Australia and Europe
who are putting money in and anyone can invest

00:04:24.199 --> 00:04:25.310
in this.

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:26.310
Anyone.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.669
And it’s really exciting because we just
have to get the message out to the crowd and

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:31.400
people who get it.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.800
People who get the movie that we’re doing
and who understand it are like “Oh!

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.810
This is fun.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.350
It’s an investment, too.

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:38.350
Cool!”

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.650
Let me tell you about one investor I talked
to.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.920
I’m talking to this very wealthy fellow
(nice guy).

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.830
We’re sitting in wingback chairs next to
his private lake.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:52.960
I felt like I was in the movie CASABLANCA
with Sydney Greenstreet.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:58.950
I’m pitching him the project and he says
“Well you know this lead character…”

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:03.090
He had read the script and he’s not in the
business so he’s not used to reading scripts.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:09.810
He just used to reading flowcharts and accounting
statements and profit and loss sheets.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.970
But he reads the script and he says “You
know, in the beginning of this movie, I don’t

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:14.970
like the lead character.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.280
She’s not very likable.”

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.949
And I said “Yeah…But you like her later?”

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:20.301
And he said “Yeah, I like her by the end.”

00:05:20.301 --> 00:05:22.699
And I said “That’s the idea.

00:05:22.699 --> 00:05:24.039
She changes.

00:05:24.039 --> 00:05:29.370
Movies have an arc where a character starts
out a certain way, they go through the story

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.919
and if it’s an upbeat ending, if it’s
a comedy, they improve their life.

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:37.610
Whatever it is, they find love, they find
success, whatever it is, they find it.”

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:42.000
And he says “Right, but I don’t like her
in the beginning.”

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.870
And I said “Oh.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:44.870
Okay.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:45.870
Right.”

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:49.900
And I explained it again, what an arc was
and I’m sure a lot of the people who are

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:53.250
your readers, who go to FilmCourage.com (a
lot of them are writers).

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:54.250
They understand this.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.410
In a movie you change, there’s an arc.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:03.129
So we talked for 20 minutes like this and
he saying “But I don’t like her in the

00:06:03.129 --> 00:06:04.129
beginning.”

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:10.080
And finally I said to the guy, I said “You
know there was a character in a movie.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.610
She was very mean, she used people.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.230
She didn't care about anybody but herself.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:15.789
She was selfish.

00:06:15.789 --> 00:06:17.990
She was basically just a horrible bitch.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:23.330
And this character was a character in the
highest grossing film of all time called GONE

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:31.060
WITH THE WIND.” and this guy was in his
70’s so he knew that movie.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:35.620
And he goes “Oh…oh…”

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:41.539
And it was just surreal having a conversation
with this guy here trying to explain this

00:06:41.539 --> 00:06:42.539
to him.

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:44.289
It wasn’t his business.

00:06:44.289 --> 00:06:48.730
He didn’t understand it because he didn’t
get this business.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:53.000
Whereas when we do the crowdfunding, the equity
crowdfunding, a lot of people (even though

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.629
it’s not their business), they get it.

00:06:55.629 --> 00:06:58.000
They’ve thought about it, you know?

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.280
They’ve thought “Oh, that would be fun.”

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.460
And so they get it and it’s such a tremendous
relief when they get it.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:09.250
On our Facebook page (we have a Facebook page
for the movie THAT CHRISTMAS MOVIE on Facebook)

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:15.710
and it’s so…you know we make little posts
about the movie, who is in it, stuff like

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:16.710
that.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:21.340
And we had a sweet little grandma in Missouri.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:27.250
She posted “This is great Thumbs up!”
and it was so heartwarming to see that we

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:33.830
reached some sweet lady that we didn’t know
in the middle of nowhere and she gets us.

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:40.620
And as filmmakers and writers, there’s nothing
better than when people get what you’re

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:40.800
doing.

